      'Rule example: Score the most points by collecting matching pairs. On your turn you turn two cards face up; if they match (same rank, suits ignored), you keep the pair and take another turn; if not, you turn them face down again and the turn passes.',
      'Turn-flow example: 1. Preparation: Shuffle the deck and spread all cards face down so they do not overlap.\n2. Play: Players take turns. At your turn, turn face up any two cards from the layout. If they match (same denomination), take both cards and place them in front of you, and take another turn. If they do not match, turn them face down in place and the next player plays.\n3. Matching: With a 52-card pack, cards match if they have the same rank (e.g. two sixes, two kings); suits are ignored. There are 26 pairs.\n4. End: Play continues until every card has been taken as part of a pair. Each player scores one point per pair collected; the player with the most points wins (ties share the win).',
